Perfectly safe, because `enumerate` is as lazy as its input. `enumerate(stream)` pulls exactly one item from the stream per item it yields, attaches the next integer, and never reads ahead. On an infinite stream it produces an infinite stream of `(0, item), (1, item), …` — which is exactly what a log writer wants:

```python
for n, line in enumerate(event_stream()):
    log.write(f"{n:06d} {line}
")
    if shutdown_requested():
        break
```

The termination is the loop's business (here, an external event), as it must be with any infinite source — `enumerate` neither adds nor removes safety.

Three details that matter in practice. First, `enumerate` counts *from where the iterator currently is*: if something already pulled three items from the stream, your numbering starts at the fourth item while claiming it is number 0. If sequence numbers must reflect the source's true position, enumerate at the source, before anything else consumes. Second, the counter is a plain Python int — it will not overflow, so multi-year streams are fine. Third, when you need the index to *drive* behavior rather than merely label — "every 100th item", "stop after index 10,000" — `enumerate` plus a condition, or `itertools.count` zipped with the stream, both work; prefer `count` when the index is the primary value and the items are secondary, since `zip(count(), stream)` makes that priority visible and stops when the stream does.

